  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is a hydrograph?",
      "a": "A hydrograph is a graph of river discharge against time at one point. A storm hydrograph shows the rising limb, the peak, the falling limb and the base flow.",
      "asks": [
        "Define hydrograph",
        "How do you read a storm hydrograph?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is lag time?",
      "a": "Lag time is the time between the centre of the rainfall and the peak discharge. Here the rain centre is at 3 hours and the peak at 8, so the lag is 5 hours.",
      "asks": [
        "Define lag time"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is base flow?",
      "a": "Base flow is the steady part of river flow fed by groundwater seeping into the channel, which continues between storms.",
      "asks": [
        "What keeps rivers flowing without rain?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do urban areas flood faster?",
      "a": "Roofs, roads and drains stop water soaking in and carry it quickly to rivers, so the hydrograph peak is higher and comes much sooner.",
      "asks": [
        "Effect of urbanisation on hydrograph"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the rational method?",
      "a": "A simple formula for peak runoff from small catchments: Q equals C i A over 3.6, with C the runoff coefficient, i the rain intensity in millimetres per hour and A the area in square kilometres, giving Q in cubic metres per second.",
      "asks": [
        "Q = CiA",
        "Runoff coefficient"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "In the rational method Q = CiA/3.6, with i in mm/h and A in km², the peak flow Q comes out in m³/s.",
    "L. K. Sherman introduced the unit hydrograph method in 1932."
  ],
